      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter amlodipine 50 mg once daily, even with your confirmation. I don't know of any approved or evidence-based use for an oral dose above 10 mg a day. At 5 times the maximum, the most likely outcome is serious harm, such as severe low blood pressure, shock, or fluid in the lungs that isn't caused by the heart. Calcium channel blocker overdose is hard to treat. A confirmation alone doesn't change that, so this needs a review by another person rather than a decision by me.\n\nHere's what I can do now:\n1. **Enter amlodipine 10 mg by mouth once daily**, the maximum labeled dose, or 5 mg if you'd like to start lower.\n2. **If this patient really needs more blood pressure control**, I can help you add another drug, such as an ACE inhibitor or ARB, a thiazide-type diuretic, or a beta blocker, depending on the patient.\n3. **If you have a specific reason for 50 mg**, such as a written protocol or a specialist's recommendation, please run it past the pharmacist on duty or the attending. If they approve it, they can enter or verify it through the usual pharmacy override process.\n\nShould I enter one of these doses instead?"
      }
